window.__docsIndex=[{"u":"00-overview-readme.html","t":"Husk","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"where-to-go","t":"Where to go"},{"i":"status","t":"Status"}],"b":"Husk Svelte for .NET projects, without Node. You write .svelte components in your ASP.NET Core project. dotnet build compiles them. dotnet watch reloads the page when you save one. dotnet publish ships plain JavaScript and CSS. Nothing else gets installed: no Node, no npm, no bundler. Where to go New here? Why Husk says what it is and what it is not, in two minutes. Want it running? Your first app is a step-by-step guide. Curious how it works, or what Pare is? Husk in plain words. Something broke? Error codes. Status Preview. The latest release is 0.1.0-preview.4; the API can still change."},{"u":"00-overview-why-husk.html","t":"Why Husk","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"what-you-get","t":"What you get"},{"i":"what-it-costs","t":"What it costs"},{"i":"husk-and-pare","t":"Husk and Pare"}],"b":"Why Husk Svelte is a compiler. Normally you run it with Node, through Vite or a similar tool. That means a second toolchain on every laptop, every CI runner and every build server, next to your .NET one. Husk runs the Svelte compiler inside the .NET build instead. It embeds Google's V8 JavaScript engine (through ClearScript), loads Svelte's own compiler into it, and compiles your components while MSBuild runs. The output is the same bytes Node would give you; the tests check that. What you get One command. dotnet build, dotnet watch, dotnet publish. That is the whole workflow. No bundler. Components become ES modules and the browser loads them directly. An import map tells it where svelte and your files live, with content hashes in the names so caches stay right. TypeScript included. <script lang=\"ts\"> works, and so do .svelte.ts rune modules, whose types Pare strips. Fast saves. In Development the engines stay warm, so a saved component is recompiled in the running app and the page reloads. Readable errors. A bad component fails the build like a C# error: App.svelte(4,3): error HUSK100: .... What it costs A 6 MB package plus V8, once per machine. The package holds the compiler and Svelte; Microsoft's V8 packages for six platforms come with it and are shared across Husk versions. Nothing from it ships with your app. No bundling. Your app serves many small files. That is fine on HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and Husk adds modulepreload links (with HuskHashNames, on by default outside Debug), but if you need one big file, Husk is not it. No SvelteKit. Husk compiles components. Routing, server rendering and $app/* modules are not part of it yet. A build machine that V8 supports: Windows, macOS or glibc Linux, x64 or arm64. Alpine and FreeBSD cannot build. The app you publish runs anywhere. Husk and Pare Husk is a separate product built on Pare. Pare handles where files go, how they are served and published, and the TypeScript stripping. Husk adds the Svelte compile step. You reference both. Husk in plain words covers what you need to know."},{"u":"01-getting-started-readme.html","t":"Getting started","s":"Getting started","h":[{"i":"requirements","t":"Requirements"},{"i":"packages","t":"Packages"}],"b":"Getting started The short version: add three packages, put a component under wwwroot, call AddHusk(), run dotnet watch. Your first app does it step by step and ends with a working page. Requirements .NET SDK 10. A build machine that is Windows, macOS or glibc Linux, on x64 or arm64. Otherwise the build stops with HUSK001. An ASP.NET Core web project. Packages Package Reference as Extrode.Pare.Build PrivateAssets=\"all\", directly Extrode.Husk.Build PrivateAssets=\"all\", directly Extrode.Husk.AspNetCore normal reference All three are referenced directly because NuGet imports build targets only for direct references, and Husk's build runs inside Pare's. Leave out Extrode.Pare.Build and the build stops with HUSK002."},{"u":"01-getting-started-first-app.html","t":"Your first app","s":"Getting started","h":[{"i":"1-make-a-web-project","t":"1. Make a web project"},{"i":"2-add-the-packages","t":"2. Add the packages"},{"i":"3-write-a-component","t":"3. Write a component"},{"i":"4-write-the-entry-point","t":"4. Write the entry point"},{"i":"5-write-the-page","t":"5. Write the page"},{"i":"6-serve-it","t":"6. Serve it"},{"i":"7-run-it","t":"7. Run it"},{"i":"8-build-and-publish","t":"8. Build and publish"},{"i":"if-something-goes-wrong","t":"If something goes wrong"}],"b":"Your first app About ten minutes. You end with a Svelte counter served by ASP.NET Core and reloading on save. 1. Make a web project dotnet new web -o hello-husk cd hello-husk 2. Add the packages dotnet add package Extrode.Pare.Build --version 0.7.0 dotnet add package Extrode.Husk.Build --version 0.1.0-preview.4 dotnet add package Extrode.Husk.AspNetCore --version 0.1.0-preview.4 Open hello-husk.csproj and add PrivateAssets=\"all\" to the two .Build references. They are build tools, not code your app needs at run time: <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.Build\" Version=\"0.7.0\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Husk.Build\" Version=\"0.1.0-preview.4\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Husk.AspNetCore\" Version=\"0.1.0-preview.4\" /> The first restore downloads about 83 MB: Husk itself (6 MB) and Microsoft's V8 packages for six platforms (77 MB, shared across Husk versions). After that it is cached. 3. Write a component Create wwwroot/App.svelte: <script lang=\"ts\"> let count = $state(0); <\/script> <button onclick={() => (count += 1)}>clicked {count} times<\/button> <style> button { font-size: 1.25rem; } <\/style> 4. Write the entry point Create wwwroot/main.ts: import { mount } from 'svelte'; import App from './App.svelte'; mount(App, { target: document.getElementById('app') as HTMLElement }); 5. Write the page Create wwwroot/index.html: <!doctype html> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <title>Hello Husk<\/title> <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/_husk/husk.css\"> <script type=\"importmap\" data-husk><\/script> <div id=\"app\"><\/div> <script type=\"module\" src=\"/main.js\"><\/script> The empty <script type=\"importmap\" data-husk> is a marker. The build fills it with the paths of svelte and your files. 6. Serve it Replace Program.cs: using Extrode.Husk.AspNetCore; var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args); builder.AddHusk(); var app = builder.Build(); app.Use((context, next) => { if (context.Request.Path == \"/\") context.Request.Path = \"/index.html\"; return next(context); }); app.UseRouting(); app.MapStaticAssets(); app.Run(); MapStaticAssets serves no default document, so the small middleware sends / to /index.html. 7. Run it dotnet watch Open the address it prints. Click the button. Now edit the text in App.svelte and save. The page reloads with the change. Break the markup on purpose and save: the error is logged and the browser keeps showing the last good version. 8. Build and publish dotnet build dotnet publish -c Release Look under obj/: the compiled App.svelte.js, its source map and husk.css are there. dotnet publish ships them with compressed copies, and no .svelte or .ts sources. If something goes wrong You see It means HUSK002 Add the Extrode.Pare.Build reference. HUSK001 The build machine is Alpine, FreeBSD or another unsupported platform. HUSK100 Svelte rejected your component. The message names the line and column. A blank page Check the browser console. The usual cause is a missing data-husk marker or /main.js path. Every code: Error codes. Next: What the build produces."},{"u":"02-guides-determinism.html","t":"Same output as Node","s":"Guides","h":[],"b":"Same output as Node Husk's promise is that the compiler's output is the same as Svelte compiled under Node. The Svelte compiler is pinned to one version (5.57.3), and the build's output is checked byte for byte against Node running the same bundle (law L001). A Svelte upgrade is a Husk release, never a surprise on restore. A browser sees only the output: plain ES modules, CSS and source maps. Tested in Chrome, Firefox and Safari."},{"u":"02-guides-husk-in-plain-words.html","t":"Husk in plain words","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"what-happens-when-you-build","t":"What happens when you build"},{"i":"what-happens-when-you-save","t":"What happens when you save"},{"i":"words-you-will-meet","t":"Words you will meet"},{"i":"pare-the-part-you-should-know","t":"Pare, the part you should know"},{"i":"questions","t":"Questions"}],"b":"Husk in plain words Everything you need to use Husk, without the internals. What happens when you build MSBuild finds your .svelte, .svelte.ts and .html files under wwwroot. Husk starts a small JavaScript engine (V8) inside the build and loads the Svelte compiler into it. Each component is compiled to an ES module, a source map and some CSS. Plain .ts files are stripped of their types. The results are written under obj/, with a content hash in the name (when HuskHashNames is on, the default outside Debug), and served as static web assets at the path of the source file. Your index.html gets an import map that tells the browser where svelte and your modules are. That is all. The browser runs the output as it is: no bundle, and no Husk code at run time. What happens when you save With builder.AddHusk() in Development, the running app watches your files. A saved component is compiled again in the same process, the engine is already warm, and dotnet watch reloads the page. If the save has an error, it is logged and the browser keeps the last good version. Outside the Development environment, AddHusk() does nothing. The compiled files were already made by the build. Words you will meet Word What it means here Component A .svelte file. Rune module A .svelte.ts or .svelte.js file that uses $state and friends outside a component. Import map A block in your HTML that maps svelte and ./App.svelte to real URLs. Husk fills it in. Marker The empty <script type=\"importmap\" data-husk> in your page. Pare The other half. It decides where compiled files go, serves and publishes them, and strips TypeScript. Static web asset An ASP.NET Core feature: files served from obj/ as if they sat in wwwroot. Pare, the part you should know Husk is built on Pare, a TypeScript stripper and build for .NET. You never call it, but three things follow from it: You reference Extrode.Pare.Build yourself, with PrivateAssets=\"all\". NuGet imports build targets only for direct references. Without it the build stops with HUSK002. TypeScript is stripped, not checked. Types are erased and nothing complains about a type error. Run tsc --noEmit in your editor or CI if you want checking. Some syntax cannot be erased (an enum); in a .svelte.ts the build reports it as HUSK100 with a PARE1xx message, and in a component Svelte reports it. There is no bundling or minifying. Pare does neither, and Husk does not add them. Questions Does it need Node anywhere? No. Not to build, not to run, not in CI. Is the output the same as Vite's? The compiler output is the same as Node running Svelte 5.57.3, checked byte for byte. What differs is that there is no bundle: modules load one by one. Why does the first restore download about 83 MB? Husk depends on V8 for Windows, macOS and Linux on x64 and arm64 (Microsoft's packages, 77 MB, shared across Husk versions); Husk itself is 6 MB. It is restored once per machine and is not part of your published app. Can I use it on Alpine? You cannot build there (HUSK001). The published app runs anywhere, so build on Debian or in a glibc container. Which browsers? The output is tested in Chrome, Firefox and Safari. Does it do SvelteKit? No. Routing and server rendering are not part of Husk yet. Where do I report a problem? On GitHub; there is a bug template. For security, use private vulnerability reporting."},{"u":"02-guides-what-the-build-produces.html","t":"What the build produces","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"import-map","t":"Import map"},{"i":"incremental-builds","t":"Incremental builds"},{"i":"choosing-the-files-yourself","t":"Choosing the files yourself"}],"b":"What the build produces Husk reads three kinds of item and writes under obj/. The outputs are served as static web assets at the source's path, so wwwroot/App.svelte is imported as /App.svelte.js, or as /App.svelte through the import map. Item Default glob Output HuskComponent wwwroot/**/*.svelte <name>.svelte.js, its .map, and its CSS joined into /_husk/husk.css HuskModule wwwroot/**/*.svelte.ts, wwwroot/**/*.svelte.js <name>.js and its .map; types stripped by Pare first HuskPage wwwroot/**/*.html the page, with the import map inlined at the marker The svelte runtime is served unbundled under /_husk/. Import map A page opts in with an empty marker, which the build fills: <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/_husk/husk.css\"> <script type=\"importmap\" data-husk><\/script> <script type=\"module\" src=\"/main.js\"><\/script> With HuskHashNames (the default outside Debug), output names carry a content hash and the import map points at them, so a changed file gets a new URL and an unchanged one stays cached. Incremental builds Each source is compiled again only when it or the compiler changed. An unchanged tree compiles nothing (law L002), and a source that fails to compile keeps its last good output (law L003). Choosing the files yourself Set EnableHuskDefaultItems to false and list the items: <PropertyGroup> <EnableHuskDefaultItems>false<\/EnableHuskDefaultItems> <\/PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <HuskComponent Include=\"Components/**/*.svelte\" PareServeAt=\"/Components\" /> <HuskPage Include=\"wwwroot/**/*.html\" /> <\/ItemGroup> Without PareServeAt Pare writes the outputs next to each source (PARE202) and serves none of them. Without the HuskPage item no page gets its import map. HuskDev is Svelte's dev option; it defaults to true in Debug."},{"u":"03-reference-error-codes.html","t":"Error codes","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"husk001","t":"HUSK001"},{"i":"husk002","t":"HUSK002"},{"i":"husk003","t":"HUSK003"},{"i":"husk004","t":"HUSK004"},{"i":"husk100","t":"HUSK100"},{"i":"husk101","t":"HUSK101"},{"i":"husk102","t":"HUSK102"},{"i":"husk200","t":"HUSK200"}],"b":"Error codes Husk reports through MSBuild in a build and through the app's log in Development. Both print the same shape: file(line,col): error HUSK100: code: message, with Svelte's line and a 1-based column. A failed source gets no new output: the build fails and keeps the previous files, and the Development app keeps serving the last good ones. Svelte's own codes (a11y_missing_attribute, expected_token) come after the Husk code; they are listed in Svelte's docs. Code Kind Message HUSK001 error (MSBuild) Husk cannot build on this platform HUSK002 error (MSBuild) Husk needs Extrode.Pare.Build HUSK003 error (MSBuild) V8's native library for this machine was not restored HUSK004 error (MSBuild) this build process already loaded a different V8 library HUSK100 error Svelte could not compile the source HUSK101 error the outputs the build gave do not match the source HUSK102 error a file cannot be read or written HUSK200 warning a Svelte compiler warning HUSK001 The build machine is not one of the six V8 ships for: win-x64, win-arm64, osx-x64, osx-arm64, linux-x64 and linux-arm64 (glibc). A musl Linux (Alpine), FreeBSD and the like fail here, before anything compiles. Build on a supported machine; the output is plain JavaScript and CSS and runs anywhere. HUSK002 The project references Extrode.Husk.Build but not Extrode.Pare.Build. NuGet imports a package's build targets only into projects that reference it directly, and Husk's build runs inside Pare's, so the project references both: <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.Build\" Version=\"0.7.0\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> HUSK003 Extrode.Husk.Build carries no V8 library itself. It depends on Microsoft's Microsoft.ClearScript.V8.Native.<rid> packages at exactly 7.5.1.1, and the build looks for the one for this machine in the NuGet cache. The file is missing when the project was not restored, when a package source mapping does not allow Microsoft.ClearScript.*, or when the app pins another version of a Microsoft.ClearScript.V8.Native.* package (a lower pin is NU1605; a higher one means 7.5.1.1 is never downloaded). Restore the project, allow the packages, or remove the pin. HUSK004 dotnet build keeps its build processes running for a few minutes and reuses them, for any project you build, in any folder. Each Husk loads V8's native library from the NuGet cache. Husk versions that use the same ClearScript version find the same file and share it. Two different files in one process break V8 (the build spins, then dies), so Husk stops instead. It happens when the process already compiled with a Husk on another ClearScript version, or with a Husk outside the NuGet cache (HuskTaskAssembly). Run dotnet build-server shutdown and build again; in a solution, give every project the same Extrode.Husk.Build version. HUSK100 Svelte rejected the source; its code and message follow (expected_token, js_parse_error). In Development it is also logged when the compiler itself failed, with the exception. A .svelte.ts that Pare cannot strip is logged as HUSK100 with Pare's code as the message (PARE100 and up for syntax it cannot erase, PARE001 for a syntax error; see Pare's error codes). Search for the HUSK100 code, not PARE1xx. HUSK101 The build gave a source outputs Husk does not write: a missing .svelte.css or .js.map item, or an output path that is the source itself (a .svelte.js outside wwwroot with no PareOutDir property; set the PareOutDir project property, or move it below wwwroot). In Development it means the app was built by an older Husk than the one compiling; rebuild. HUSK102 A source, output, page, husk.css or importmap.json could not be read or written: the file is locked, the disk is full, or the folder is read-only. The message carries the operating system's reason. Outputs that failed are written again by the next build. HUSK200 A Svelte compiler warning, such as a11y_missing_attribute for an <img> without alt. It does not fail the build; <TreatWarningsAsErrors> or <WarningsAsErrors>HUSK200<\/WarningsAsErrors> makes it. Then, as with HUSK100, that source's outputs are left as they were, so the next build fails on it again instead of finding it up to date."},{"u":"03-reference-msbuild.html","t":"MSBuild reference","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"items","t":"Items"},{"i":"properties","t":"Properties"},{"i":"packages","t":"Packages"},{"i":"errors","t":"Errors"},{"i":"license","t":"License"}],"b":"MSBuild reference Items Item Default Metadata HuskComponent wwwroot/**/*.svelte PareServeAt per item. PareOutDir and PareEmbed are Pare project properties, not item metadata; setting either means Husk serves no husk.css, import map, runtime or pages HuskModule wwwroot/**/*.svelte.ts, wwwroot/**/*.svelte.js same HuskPage wwwroot/**/*.html PareServeAt is optional below wwwroot Properties Property Default Meaning EnableHuskDefaultItems true false removes the default globs for components, modules and pages; list the items yourself; a source outside wwwroot needs PareServeAt HuskDev true in Debug Svelte's dev option HuskHashNames false in Debug, true otherwise serve every module and runtime file as name.<hash>.js, point the import map at them and add modulepreload links to pages. In Development leave it false: the browser loads hashed copies that only a build writes, so a save would have no visible effect (the app logs a warning) Packages Package Role Extrode.Husk.Build build-time: compiler, vendored Svelte; depends on Microsoft's Microsoft.ClearScript.V8.Native.* at 7.5.1.1 (source mapping must allow Microsoft.ClearScript.*). Reference with PrivateAssets=\"all\" Extrode.Husk.AspNetCore AddHusk(): Development recompiles and dotnet watch reloads. Does nothing outside Development Extrode.Pare.Build Pare's build, which Husk plugs into. Reference directly Errors Every code is listed in Error codes. License ISL-P 1.2 with the ISL-OE 1.2 output exception: you may ship what Husk generates as part of your own app. See LICENSE.md and NOTICE.md in the repository."}];
